WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
Everybody has some issues with procrastination,

2
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:06.000
because if you're working on something,

3
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.000
it means you're not working on a lot of other things.

4
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:14.000
But some people have more issues with procrastination than others.

5
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:19.000
In this video, we're going to give you a little insight into procrastination,

6
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:21.000
why it arises,

7
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:26.000
and a powerful little tool to help you address it.

8
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:30.000
When you look at something that you'd really rather not do,

9
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.000
it seems that you activate the areas of your brain associated with pain.

10
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:37.000
Your brain, naturally enough,

11
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:41.000
looks for a way to stop that negative stimulation

12
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:44.000
by switching your attention to something else.

13
00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:46.000
But here's the trick.

14
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:50.000
Researchers discovered that not long after people might start

15
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:53.000
actually working on what they didn't like,

16
00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:57.000
that neural discomfort disappeared.

17
00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:01.000
So it seems what happens when you procrastinate is something like this.

18
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:07.000
First, you observe and get a cue about something that causes a tiny bit of unease.

19
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:09.000
You don't like it.

20
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:11.000
So to make the sensation go away,

21
00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:15.000
you turn your attention from whatever caused that unease.

22
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.000
You turn toward something more pleasant.

23
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:21.000
The result? You feel happier.

24
00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:23.000
Temporarily.

25
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:26.000
We're going to talk more about procrastination later on,

26
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:31.000
but in the meantime, I'm going to let you in on a handy little mental tool.

27
00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:34.000
This tool is called the Pomodoro.

28
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:39.000
It was invented by Francesco Cirillo in the early 1980s.

29
00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:43.000
Pomodoro is Italian for tomato.

30
00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:47.000
The timer you use often looks like a tomato,

31
00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:53.000
but really, a timer is all there is to this elegant little technique.

32
00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:57.000
All you need to do is set a timer to 25 minutes,

33
00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:02.000
turn off all interruptions, and then focus.

34
00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:03.000
That's it.

35
00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:06.000
Most anybody can focus for 25 minutes.

36
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:10.000
You may be surprised to learn that the most important thing

37
00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:16.000
is what you do after you finish those 25 minutes of focus.

38
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:23.000
At that point, take from 2 to 5 minutes to retrieve what you've just learned.

39
00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:29.000
That means you recall the most important ideas or concepts you've just learned

40
00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:35.000
by pulling the ideas, as much as you can, from your own mind.

41
00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:40.000
Only peek at what you've been studying if you have to.

42
00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:46.000
This type of retrieval practice strengthens the memory traces,

43
00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:50.000
making them much easier to find later.

44
00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:55.000
After you've retrieved the most important parts of what you've just learned,

45
00:02:55.000 --> 00:03:04.000
give yourself another 2 or 3 minutes of what neuroscientists call restful wakefulness.

46
00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:10.000
During this time, above all else, do not pick up your phone.

47
00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:14.000
That jolts you instantly back into the focused mode.

48
00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:21.000
Focused mode, after all, is when you're focusing on something outside your body.

49
00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:27.000
Instead, during those last few minutes of your pomodoro,

50
00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:32.000
just rest quietly or do something simple and undemanding,

51
00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:38.000
like making a cup of tea or watching your hamster take a nap.

52
00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:45.000
What's happening here is that you're giving your brain the chance to move even more

53
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:50.000
into the diffuse mode, that more relaxed style of thinking

54
00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:54.000
that helps your mind knit together new connections.

55
00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:59.000
In the first part, after your 25 minutes of external focus,

56
00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:03.000
when you're actively retrieving what you learned,

57
00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:09.000
you're using one part of your diffuse mode system to reinforce learning.

58
00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:17.000
Then, when you rest quietly, you're using another part of your diffuse mode system.

59
00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:23.000
You're not conscious of it, but during this quiet rest period,

60
00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:30.000
you're letting your brain spontaneously replay patterns from what you just studied.

61
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:35.000
Neuroscientists have found that these short moments of awake rest

62
00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:43.000
help lock in your new learning, shifting it from fragile to lasting memory.

63
00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:51.000
So think of the pomodoro as a period of intense 25-minute period of focus

64
00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:56.000
followed by a built-in two-part recovery phase.

65
00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:00.000
Focus, retrieve, then rest,

66
00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:07.000
and you'll find your brain learning more deeply with less frustration.

67
00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:08.000
Give it a try.

68
00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:15.000
Next, we're going to see how one very shy 10-year-old changed her brain.

69
00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:22.000
♪♪♪